# /etc/cron.d/bootbench
#
# ONE line, not one per device. Every device's own `schedule:` in
# devices.yaml is evaluated inside the tick, so adding a board is a YAML
# block and nothing else -- no crontab edit, no restart.
#
#   install -m 0644 bootbench.cron /etc/cron.d/bootbench
#
# Why every 15 minutes rather than once at 01:00: the tick asks "was there a
# fire time between the last recorded tick and now?", not "is it 01:00 right
# now". So a window missed because the coordinator was down, or because the
# target's image had not landed on the share yet, is still caught later the
# same day instead of silently skipped until tomorrow.
#
# The tick is cheap and does no network I/O until it has already decided a
# device is due: at most one GET /healthz and one GET /latest-build per due
# device. A tick that finds nothing due costs a few database queries.
#
# TZ must match server.yaml's `timezone` and both systemd units. croniter
# reads local time; disagreement moves the nightly relative to when the
# build share publishes, which shows up as "it ran before the build existed".
#
# Both lines source /etc/bootbench/env by hand. cron inherits nothing from
# the systemd units, so `EnvironmentFile=` does not apply here -- and
# without BOOTBENCH_AGENT_TOKEN the tick reaches step 5, gets a 401 from the
# agent, and records `agent_unhealthy`. That is a clean skip-with-reason
# rather than an error, so the nightly just quietly never runs.
#
# The `[ -r ]` guard is not decoration: `.` is a special built-in, and in
# dash a failed `.` exits the shell outright, which would take the tick with
# it. The guard makes a missing file behave like systemd's `-` prefix.
# `set -a` is what exports the assignments into the python process.

TZ=Asia/Kolkata
MAILTO=""
PATH=/usr/bin:/bin

*/15 * * * * bootbench set -a; [ -r /etc/bootbench/env ] && . /etc/bootbench/env; set +a; cd /opt/bootbench/server && .venv/bin/python main.py tick >> /var/log/bootbench/tick.log 2>&1

# Retention. Nothing else trims anything: the skill's own report caps itself
# at 30 runs, but the pulled log trees are unbounded and a night is tens of
# MB per device.
17 4 * * * bootbench set -a; [ -r /etc/bootbench/env ] && . /etc/bootbench/env; set +a; cd /opt/bootbench/server && .venv/bin/python main.py gc >> /var/log/bootbench/gc.log 2>&1
